as of last week the paragon team pushed  out what's likely to be their final  update on April 26th Epic Games will  abandon its free-to-play MOBA Paragon  has been in early access in open beta  for over two years now it built a player  base of more than five million players  and a development team that pushed out  well over 100 updates and now the game  is effectively canceled after a rocky  start gaming outlets claimed it had made  a comeback but clearly not a lasting one  where did it go wrong  [Music]  Paragon was announced in 2015 as a  mobile for PlayStation 4 and PC epic set  out to make a MOBA that embraced  third-person action elements with an  emphasis on mobility the developer  wanted to showcase the power of its  in-house Unreal 4 engine while pushing  the boundaries of the genre the idea was  to merge the fast-paced action of  third-person shooters with the strategy  and team compositions of a MOBA in 2016  Paragon went into paid early access with  13 heroes and one large map players  could finally experience one of the  best-looking MOBAs to date while the  game eventually transitioned to  free-to-play at the start you could buy  a $20 founders pack a $60 challengers  founder pack or a $100 masters founder  pack all of these versions gave you  access to the game and a slew of digital  content to get you started  Paragon saw some success but not without  its share of issues due to the large map  size players needed to be able to move  quickly so the team introduced a travel  mode speed this allowed players to  toggle between two movement speeds one  to get around the map and the other for  combat it proved helpful getting around  the map however players would use it to  easily flee resulting in frustrating  engagements despite this the dev team  was transparent and constantly looking  for feedback from the community they  continually tweaked and updated the  growing cast of heroes eventually  capping it at 38  [Music]  in August 2017 the game left early  access and entered a free-to-play open  beta however fans could purchase a  physical version of the game called the  essentials Edition for $60 which  included the beta and a bunch of in-game  currency and other digital goodies from  this point forward Paragon went through  a series of changes in an attempt to  streamline matches the map size was  decreased players were more  conventionally funneled into lanes to  increase engagements and the movement  speed was continuously tweaked in order  to find a sweet spot however instead of  really fixing the movement problem epic  introduced a new smaller map called  monolith that replaced the original  welcome to the monolith update bringing  you the new battleground in Paragon  compared to our legacy map monolith is  more compact easier to navigate and  features faster paced action than ever  before the loadout system that allowed  players to equip six modifiable cards  was reduced to three cards that couldn't  be modified this was an attempt to make  it more approachable once these changes  were implemented players took notice and  some express concerns after that the  player count began to drop and this  downward spiral continued due to  balancing issues the diehard fans who  had spent hours fine-tuning loadouts  mixing and matching different cards and  learning all the ins and outs were left  by the wayside but above all fans were  torn on the new map some liked it  because it was quicker paced but others  felt it ruin the strategy to make  matters worse due to movement speed  changes the original map was removed  altogether epic tried to make Paragon  more accessible but in doing so they  alienated the player base that was  propping the game up streamlining  systems to reach a broader audience  isn't inherently a bad thing  monster hunter world has seen a lot of  success thanks to this but Capcom wasn't  constantly rewriting the script while  hundreds of thousands of people were  playing sure sweeping changes and  overhauls it to be expected when it  comes to early access but the changes  fans endured with Paragon seemed like  attempts to chase trends triple-a  publishers and developers are always  looking for the next big thing but at  what point does a game like Paragon or a  developer like epic lose its identity  [Music]  here we are in 2018 and Paragon is  coming to an end there are many factors  that could have spelled the death of  Paragon but the most obvious and most  likely is the recent success of  fortnight a game that oddly enough was  following a similar marketing courses  Paragon the game launched with a paid  early access beta version and will  eventually become free-to-play this year  however fortnight's boom in popularity  wasn't necessarily due to the game that  epic shipped back in july it's thanks to  battle royale a free-to-play version of  fortnight that has recently hit 45  million players and two million  concurrent's an absurd number that blows  Paragon out of the water but once again  is a battle royale mode true to  fortnight is it just another example of  epic chasing trends with Paragons  dwindling numbers and fortnight's  explosion I have to wonder did epic  shift resources work power and time to  its star player only to neglect the game  that so many veterans loved but not  everyone thinks that fortnight's Rises  what led to paragons demise one redditor  the rough stuff has tracked 10-cents  involvement with epic games Tencent is a  massive and I mean massive Chinese  investing company that some 330 million  into epic games which translates to  nearly half its shares but what does  that have to do with Paragon while  Tencent is also the primary shareholder  of riot the developer behind League of  Legends the rough stuff claims that once  Paragon started to pick up steam Tencent  took notice and became more and more  invested with the project so it wouldn't  become a direct competitor with League  of Legends rather than pitting it  directly against League measures were  taken to make Paragon more casual in  order to compete with heroes of the  storm and smite I'm paraphrasing the  rough stuffs long in-depth breakdown of  10-cents involvement but this could have  played a role in Paragons fall well I  don't think Paragon would have come  close to league's popularity and may  have had the potential to pull some of  the players due to its next-gen feel and  fast paced  action personally I think it was a bit  of both Paragon had been revamped so  many times with inconsistent results  while fortnight struck gold with its  battle royale mode almost immediately  there's no telling what epic could have  done with Paragon to even reach a  fraction of fortnight's popularity it's  hard to say how much epic is made off  these two games but 45,000,000 has a  much better ring to it than 5-million  [Music]  so what's left of Paragon even though  these last few months have been  tumultuous Paragon has built up a  community of dedicated fans some  invested hundreds of hours hundreds of  dollars filled the forums with tons of  feedback and created community  tournaments and fan sites although the  fanbase was an enormous it was resilient  it was pulled a ton of different  directions over the course of two years  as epic continually reworked the game  and refocused its direction the good  news is that epic is refunding players  who invested more than time into the  game unfortunately the process is  arduous and time-consuming asking you to  provide receipts dates and item  descriptions but a mere refund doesn't  account for the hours the community  spent beta testing the game in supplying  epic with a constant stream of feedback  over the years it's hard not to feel  burned after all of that Paragon wasn't  just a game that was canceled after a  few trailers or a game can shortly after  an early access period it was a triple a  game that built up a community with a  two-year early access beta test and then  shuddered before it came to fruition  Star Wars Galaxies and halo 2 have  suffered similar fates but both those  games were live for nearly a decade  Paragon has been playable for two years  scrolling through Paragons reddit page  there are hundreds of posts from  disappointed fans some claim they won't  jump over to fortnight or any epic game  for that matter out of principle others  are recounting inside jokes and  highlights over the years and some are  even begging epic to sell the IP to  another company that will continue to  support it  I can't imagine the latter ever  happening as of now it seems like  Paragons fate is sealed  you  you
 

